==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|obsequiī||complaisant, yielding}}, alteration of {{m|la|obsequia||compliance}} (by confusion, in association with {{m|la|exequiae|exsequia|funeral rites}}, from {{m|la|exsequī||follow or accompany to the grave}}).

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK|US}} {{IPA|/ˈɔbsɪ.kwiː/|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|obsequies}}

# The last [[office]] for the [[dead]]
# {{lb|en|chiefly|in the plural}} A [[funeral]] [[rite]] or [[service]].
#* '''1919 ''' — [[w:Ronald Firbank|Ronald Firbank]], ''[[w:Valmouth|Valmouth]]'', Duckworth, hardback edition, page 13
#*: But, to-day, there were no '''obsequies''' to observe at all.

====Usage notes====
* in modern usage, it is mainly in the plural: "[[obsequies]]", and not to be confused with [[obsequious]]

